:XuiPushButton()
##############################
#####  XuiPushButton ()  #####
##############################

	XuiPushButton (@grid, #Create, x, y, width, height, window, parent)

	XuiPushButton will generate a #Selection callback message when the
	button is pushed, or released, or on both depending on the style:
	 (See: SetCallback message)

	style = 0 #Selection on MouseUp,               $$BorderRaise2 on exit
	style = 1 #Selection on MouseDown,             $$BorderRaise2 on exit
	style = 2 #Selection on MouseUp,               $$BorderFlat   on exit
	style = 3 #Selection on MouseDown,             $$BorderFlat   on exit
	style = 4 #Selection on MouseDown and MouseUp, $$BorderRaise2 on exit
	style = 5 #Selection on MouseDown and MouseUp, $$BorderFlat   on exit

	The border type can be overridden with a #SetBorder message:
	  XuiSendMessage (grid, #SetBorder, borderExit, borderUp, borderDown, 0, kid, 0)

	When the #Selection message is generated:
	   v0 = 1 on mouseDown, and = 0 on mouseUp
	   v1 = grid number of the push button grid

	The state of the button can be checked with:
	  XuiSendMessage (grid, #GetValue, @value, 0, 0, 0, kid, 0)
	    grid,kid = target push button to be checked
	    @value returned = 1 if operated (down), = 0 if released (up)

	See: Create, CreateWindow, XuiPressButton(), XuiToggleButton(), XuiRadioButton()

FUNCTION  XuiPushButton (grid, message, v0, v1, v2, v3, r0, (r1, r1$, r1[], r1$[]))
:XuiQueueCallbacks()
##################################
#####  XuiQueueCallbacks ()  #####
##################################

		XuiQueueCallbacks() is alled by XuiCallback() to add callback messages
	to the callback queue when a program has set the address XuiQueueCallbacks()
	with the SetCallback message.
	The "convenience function message loop," in many programs, acquire the
	messages from the queue with the XuiGetNextCallback() function.

	See: SetCallback, XuiCallback(), XuiGetCallback(), XuiGetNextCallback(), XuiSetCallback()

FUNCTION  XuiQueueCallbacks (grid, message, v0, v1, v2, v3, r0, r1)
